  "nameStory": "The Yellow Gymnopilus gets its straightforward name from its distinctive golden-yellow coloration that catches the eye in the forest. The scientific name 'luteus' literally means 'yellow' in Latin, making this one of those rare cases where both the common and scientific names tell the same colorful story.",
  "description": "The Yellow Gymnopilus is a medium-sized mushroom with a bright golden-yellow cap that can reach 2-4 inches across. It grows in clusters on decaying hardwood, particularly favoring oak and other deciduous trees. This saprophytic fungus (meaning it feeds on dead organic matter) has a rusty-orange spore print that's characteristic of its genus. Like many of its rustgill relatives, it has a somewhat bitter taste and contains psychoactive compounds.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Bright golden-yellow to orange-yellow, often with a slightly sticky or tacky feel when fresh, becoming dry with age",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Start yellowish but mature to rusty-orange or rust-brown, closely spaced and attached to the stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Yellow to orange-brown, often with a partial ring or ring zone, fibrous texture that may feel slightly tough",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Rusty-orange to rust-brown, a key identifying feature for the rustgill genus",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Often has a slightly bitter or earthy odor, sometimes described as somewhat unpleasant",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Habitat",
      "description": "Always grows on decaying hardwood, typically in overlapping clusters or groups on logs, stumps, or dead branches",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Yellow Gymnopilus thrives on decaying hardwood throughout New England's deciduous and mixed forests. Look for it on fallen oak, maple, birch, and beech logs, as well as dead stumps and branches. It prefers moist woodland areas with good leaf litter and is commonly found from low elevations up into the mountains.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Rarely fruiting this early, though occasional specimens may appear in late spring during warm, wet periods on well-rotted logs.",
    "summer": "Peak fruiting season begins in mid to late summer, especially after periods of rain and humidity when the forest floor stays consistently moist.",
    "fall": "Prime season continues through fall, with the most abundant flushes typically occurring after autumn rains until the first hard frosts.",
    "winter": "Fruiting ceases with cold weather, though dried specimens from fall may persist on logs and provide identification practice."
  },
  "funFact": "This unassuming woodland mushroom contains the same psychoactive compounds (psilocybin and psilocin) found in the famous 'magic mushrooms,' yet it's often overlooked by foragers because of its bitter taste and preference for growing on rotting wood rather than in open fields.",
  "confusionNotes": "Yellow Gymnopilus is frequently confused with its showier relatives, the Spectacular Rustgill and other Gymnopilus species. The key difference is size and intensity - Yellow Gymnopilus tends to be smaller and more purely yellow, while Spectacular Rustgill is larger with more orange tones. Memory trick: 'Yellow is mellow' - the smaller, more modestly colored species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite containing psychoactive compounds, Yellow Gymnopilus is not sought after for recreational purposes due to its bitter taste and unpredictable potency. The genus name 'Gymnopilus' means 'naked cap,' referring to the smooth cap surface without scales, though this feature varies among species in the genus.",
  "compareHints": "Among its Gymnopilus relatives, Yellow Gymnopilus is distinguished by its smaller size and purer yellow coloration compared to the larger, more orange Spectacular Rustgill. It's also less scaly than the Scaly Rustgill and lacks the intensely bitter taste of the Bitter Rustgill. The consistently yellow cap color and medium size make it a 'goldilocks' species - not too big, not too small, and not too orange in the rustgill family.",
